node_modules/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
.env
.env.local
.aistudio/
.claude/settings.local.json
.DS_Store

# Agent scratch / probe files in tests/
tests/_*.js
flutter/test/shots/
flutter/test/golden/failures/

# Scratch probes written by review agents while they measure something in app/.
#
# These are throwaway by nature and must never be committed. One of them was
# named zz_probe_recovery_test.dart, and that suffix is the hazard: `flutter
# test` collects every file matching *_test.dart, so a stray probe joins the
# suite and runs in CI forever, failing on something nobody maintains. Another
# put `flutter analyze` at four issues, which would have reddened the branch
# check on the next push.
#
# Ignoring them by pattern is a guard; remembering to delete them is a rule, and
# a rule that has already been missed twice in one session.
app/test/zz_*
app/tool/_tmp_*

# THE PATTERN ABOVE WAS TOO NARROW, and it let one through on 2026-10-04.
#
# A recovery-review agent wrote `app/test/recovery_probe_test.dart` while it
# measured something, and a `git add -A` in this session swept it into a
# commit. `zz_*` did not match it, so the guard that exists precisely for this
# did nothing. Matching on the word `probe` anywhere in the name is what the
# comment above was always trying to say.
app/test/*probe*
app/test/*_scratch*

# AND AT THE app/ ROOT, which is where the next one landed.
#
# A QA agent wrote `app/zz_probe.dart` on 2026-10-04, outside both lib/ and
# test/, so neither the pattern above nor the one for test/ matched it. The
# rule keeps being written one directory too narrow, so this pair is anchored
# on the NAME rather than the place.
app/zz_*
app/*probe*.dart
